<p id="para-00027"><strong>Electronegativity and Dipole Moments</strong></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-28 Identify the most electronegative element in each of the following molecules:</p>

<p><strong>(a) </strong>CH<sub>2</sub>FCl <strong>(b) </strong>FCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br<strong> (c) </strong>HOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub><strong> (d) </strong>CH<sub>3</sub>OCH<sub>2</sub>Li</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-29 Use the electronegativity table given in Figure 2.3 to predict which bond in each of the following pairs is more polar, and indicate the direction of bond polarity for each compound.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> H<sub>3</sub>C&ndash;Cl or Cl&ndash;Cl<strong> (b) </strong>H<sub>3</sub>C&ndash;H or H&ndash;Cl<strong> (c)</strong> HO&ndash;CH<sub>3</sub> or (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>Si&ndash;CH<sub>3</sub><strong> (d) </strong>H<sub>3</sub>C&ndash;Li or Li&ndash;OH</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-30 Which of the following molecules has a dipole moment? Indicate the expected direction of each.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> <img alt="The bond-line structure has a benzene ring with a hydroxyl group at C1." style="width: 120px; height: 84px;" width="120px" height="84px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473140/551aa3556230285c9298f0a3fe8665c85cc8a2db_5nb7?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=120&amp;height=84" /> <strong>(b)</strong> <img alt="In a benzene ring, both C1 and C2 are bonded to a hydroxyl group." style="width: 120px; height: 91px;" width="120px" height="91px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473142/96c742095e5db4243f4a527a5218e428f666885a_ooni?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=120&amp;height=91" /> <strong>(c)</strong><img alt="In a benzene ring, both C1 and C3 are bonded to a hydroxyl group." style="width: 164px; height: 82px;" width="164px" height="82px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473141/9904ac808b1a7fe50653c14fa39bc33cc9ff71fa_djf8?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=164&amp;height=82" /> <strong>(d)</strong> <img alt="In a benzene ring, both C1 and C4 are bonded to a hydroxyl group." style="width: 170px; height: 91px;" width="170px" height="91px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473143/2a6dc08a03aacda808beca5542d60955417ad53e_20zd?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=170&amp;height=91" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-31</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> The H&ndash;Cl bond length is 136 pm. What would the dipole moment of HCl be if the molecule were 100% ionic, H<b><sup>+</sup></b> Cl<b><sup>&minus;</sup></b>?</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> The actual dipole moment of HCl is 1.08 D. What is the percent ionic character of the H&ndash;Cl bond?</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-32 Phosgene, Cl<sub>2</sub>C=O, has a smaller dipole moment than formaldehyde, H<sub>2</sub>C==O, even though it contains electronegative chlorine atoms in place of hydrogen. Explain.</p>

