<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-59 We&rsquo;ll see in the next chapter that organic molecules can be classified according to the functional groups they contain, where a functional group is a collection of atoms with a characteristic chemical reactivity. Use the electronegativity values given in Figure 2.3 to predict the direction of polarization of the following functional groups.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> <img alt="Ketone has a central carbonyl group with two open single bonds." style="width: 69px; height: 120px;" width="69px" height="120px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473181/b1096bce177d9e245a73a8f6de2bcffe1c26d0b8_v9yv?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=69&amp;height=120" /> <strong>(b)</strong><img alt="Alcohol has a carbon atom with three open single bonds bonded to a hydroxyl group." style="width: 87px; height: 119px;" width="87px" height="119px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473182/2c934e47eb99eed57aa27099e1487848fa288327_sn2o?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=87&amp;height=119" /> <strong>(c)</strong><img alt="Amide has a carbonyl group bonded to an open single bond and a primary amine group." style="width: 99px; height: 120px;" width="99px" height="120px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473183/866b22901c074148dc105fd8b0363bb9e2c0b0f3_5n83?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=99&amp;height=120" /> <strong>(d)</strong> <img alt="Nitrile has a carbon atom with an open single bond, and a triple bond to a nitrogen atom." style="width: 81px; height: 122px;" width="81px" height="122px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473185/22587d04e0113abb51992937853e3d3d25348d86_t6cj?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=81&amp;height=122" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-60 The <i>azide</i> functional group, which occurs in azidobenzene, contains three adjacent nitrogen atoms. One resonance structure for azidobenzene is shown. Draw three additional resonance structures, and assign appropriate formal charges to the atoms in all four.</p>

<p><img alt="The structure of Azidobenzene features two lone pair on the nitrogen bonded to benzene and one lone pair on the terminal triple bonded nitrogen." style="width: 363px; height: 132px;" width="363px" height="132px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473184/daf6a854c6badde892a4378dd9e3a866b5bc51c6_8imr?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=363&amp;height=132" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-61 Phenol, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH, is a stronger acid than methanol, CH<sub>3</sub>OH, even though both contain an O&ndash;H bond. Draw the structures of the anions resulting from loss of H<sup>+</sup> from phenol and methanol, and use resonance structures to explain the difference in acidity.</p>

<p><img alt="The structures of phenol and methanol with their respective p K a values, 9.89 and 15.54." style="width: 444px; height: 146px;" width="444px" height="146px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473186/f798871ac966a9fc8e28bd373521cfc252f3b652_q0ne?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=444&amp;height=146" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-62 Thiamin diphosphate (TPP), a derivative of vitamin B<sub>1</sub> required for glucose metabolism, is a weak acid that can be deprotonated by a base. Assign formal charges to the appropriate atoms in both TPP and its deprotonation product.</p>

<p><img alt="Thiamin diphosphate reacts with base forming a product, in which the hydrogen (with p K a 18) at C2 of the 5-membered ring is replaced with a lone pair." style="width: 920px; height: 234px;" width="920px" height="234px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473187/df5d6d277e742148bc0ebb8f3071999e655257a2_wa39?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=920&amp;height=234" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-63 Which of the following compounds or ions have a dipole moment?</p>

<p><b>(a)</b>&ensp;Carbonate ion (CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>&ndash;</sup>)&emsp;<b>(b)</b>&ensp;<img alt="An oxygen atom with two single bonds carries two lone pairs." height="35" width="72" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473188/7cfc39f868ea1103612767aa8ab6be3a6d512c26_4ute?revision=1" />&emsp;<b>(c)</b>&ensp;<img alt="A condensed formula reads, carbon with a positive charge bonded to three methyl groups." style="width: 76px; height: 46px;" width="76px" height="46px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473189/e2a306a8409c1fcb5bf960859f657ccaa036442d_r76o?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=76&amp;height=46" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-64 Use the p<i>K</i><sub>a</sub> table in Appendix B to determine in which direction the equilibrium is favored.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> <img alt="In a reversible reaction, phenol reacts with benzene bonded to carboxylate ion to form phenoxide ion and benzoic acid." style="width: 676px; height: 90px;" width="676px" height="90px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473190/b33c605f18ef9fa62d1094e1ba9dc9d145e6bbf0_hl77?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=676&amp;height=90" /></p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> <img alt="In a reversible reaction, propanol reacts with a negatively charged amine to form a 3-carbon chain bonded to an oxygen ion and ammonia." style="width: 552px; height: 42px;" width="552px" height="42px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473192/fd617e9a940cc1e6cbac99237c190b434dbcea63_rn99?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=552&amp;height=42" /></p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> <img alt="In a reversible reaction, a methyl ion reacts with nitromethane to form methane and a methylene group with a negative charge bonded to a nitro group." style="width: 449px; height: 43px;" width="449px" height="43px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/473191/7e03d99075c8f32b836340fdebd6e1ea55e7f479_ej0i?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=449&amp;height=43" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM2</strong>-65 Which intermolecular force is predominantly responsible for each observation below?</p>

<p><strong>(a) </strong>CH<sub>3</sub>(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>29</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, a component found in paraffin wax, is a solid at room temperature while CH<sub>3</sub>(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>6</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> is a liquid.</p>

<p><strong>(b) </strong>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH has a higher boiling point than CH<sub>4</sub>.</p>

<p><strong>(c) </strong>CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>H, which is found in vinegar, will dissolve in water but not in oil. Assume that oil is CH<sub>3</sub>(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>.</p>
